# Nexus infra-service registry — declarative input to
# `monitor/bootstrap-recover.sh`.
#
# Copy this file to `monitor/services.registry` (operator-local,
# gitignored) and list the long-running infra services your deployment
# expects to be up. On a restart the orchestrator runs
# `bootstrap-recover.sh`, which relaunches each registered service that
# is BOTH unhealthy AND has no tmux window. Adding a new service is a
# one-line edit here — no code change.
#
# FORMAT — one service per line, FOUR required fields plus an OPTIONAL
# fifth and sixth, separated by single TABs (not spaces):
#
#     <name>\t<workdir>\t<launch-cmd>\t<healthcheck-cmd>[\t<logfile>[\t<policy>]]
#
#   name        tmux window name to host the service (also the
#               idempotency key — a window by this name means "already
#               running, do not relaunch").
#   workdir     directory the launch + healthcheck run in. `~` and
#               `$NEXUS_ROOT` are expanded. A missing workdir means the
#               service is skipped (its project isn't checked out here).
#   launch-cmd  shell command sent to the new tmux window. Prefer a
#               supervised restart-loop wrapper (it survives crashes
#               within the session; this registry survives the session
#               itself dying).
#   healthcheck shell command run in <workdir>; exit 0 == healthy.
#               exit 100 == a FINDING (your-org/nexus-code#1423): the service is
#               ALIVE and the condition it watches is PRESENT — its stderr is the
#               finding. The watcher opens no incident, never restarts, and
#               renders "UP and reports a FINDING" (no DOWN, no restart remedy),
#               clearing itself when the check goes green. For MONITORS
#               registered as services (tmpfs-guard --check). Any other
#               non-zero == DOWN.
#
#               TWO OPTIONAL PROTOCOL LINES ON STDERR, both stripped from the
#               rendered emit. A service that writes neither behaves exactly as
#               before, so adopting them is opt-in per service:
#
#                 finding-key: <stable token>
#                   The SUPPRESSION key. Without it the whole message is the
#                   key — and a message carrying a live counter re-fires on
#                   counter noise: measured on tmpfs-guard, `entries 14129` ->
#                   `14127` re-emitted the finding two minutes later while the
#                   state a reader cares about had changed zero times. The
#                   framework cannot GUESS which part of a message is stable
#                   and a digit-stripping heuristic would merge findings that
#                   differ only in a number that matters, so the SERVICE
#                   declares it — the same way it already declares its payload
#                   by what it writes to stderr. Build it from the SITUATION
#                   (a band, a condition set), never from a measurement.
#
#                 finding-band: <non-negative integer>
#                   A monotone severity. Its one job is that a MUTE recorded at
#                   one band BREAKS when the situation crosses into a worse one.
#                   Declare none and any key change breaks the mute instead,
#                   which errs toward EMITTING — the safe direction.
#
#               MUTING A FINDING. An operator who has judged and accepted a
#               finding may silence it by writing
#               `<state-dir>/service-health/<name>.finding-mute`:
#
#                   service=tmpfs-guard
#                   owner=operator
#                   reason=other users' memory on a shared node; not ours to reap
#                   until=2026-10-01          # this or tracker= (or both)
#                   tracker=your-org/nexus-code#1423
#                   band=1                    # the band it was accepted at
#
#               Four rules, each from something that bit this repo:
#                 * it must EXPIRE — an open-ended mute is an unowned
#                   exemption (#1486, where one row records in its own note
#                   that no rationale is on file), so the SAME predicate #1482
#                   applies to a `none` row applies here: a tracker ref or an
#                   `until:` date;
#                 * it must carry an OWNER and a REASON, for the same reason;
#                 * it BREAKS when the band worsens — a mute keyed on "over
#                   threshold" would sit silently through 10% -> 77%, making
#                   the mute the mechanism by which the incident the monitor
#                   exists to catch goes unreported;
#                 * `muted` and `cleared` are DISTINGUISHABLE in the record —
#                   different files, different events, different words.
#               An INVALID or EXPIRED mute is IGNORED LOUDLY, never obeyed
#               quietly: a suppression that fails open hides the thing it was
#               asked to hide for a reason nobody stated.
#               Keep it cheap and fast (short curl timeout / pgrep /
#               test -f) — it runs on every recovery sweep.
#
#               VERIFY IDENTITY, NOT JUST LIVENESS (your-org/nexus-code#609).
#               This sandbox SHARES THE HOST NETWORK NAMESPACE, so a port is
#               a HOST-GLOBAL resource: `localhost:8765` is reachable by, and
#               bindable by, every other operator's sandbox on this machine.
#               A healthcheck that only asks "does something answer this
#               port?" therefore CANNOT distinguish YOUR service from
#               somebody else's that won a bind race — and the loser of that
#               race reports HEALTHY while serving nothing. That is not
#               theoretical: nexus-remote-ssh was down for 2h30m on
#               2026-07-29 while another operator's daemon answered its port
#               and its banner-only probe returned 0 the whole time.
#
#               So make the probe ask for something only YOUR service can
#               produce. Cheap patterns, in order of preference:
#                 * an authenticated endpoint whose token lives in your tree
#                   (monitor/jupyter-health.sh is the reference: /api/status
#                   403s without the project's own token, so another user's
#                   Jupyter on the port fails);
#                 * a body assertion on a path your service alone serves,
#                   not a bare 200 on `/`. Write it CAPTURE-THEN-MATCH:
#                       grep -q '<your-marker>' <<<"$(curl -fsS …)"
#                   and NEVER the piped form
#                       curl -fsS … | grep -q '<your-marker>'   grepq-antipattern
#                   Under `pipefail` (which bootstrap-recover.sh sets, and
#                   healthchecks inherit) `grep -q` exits at the match and
#                   SIGPIPEs curl, so the pipeline reports 141 — FAILURE —
#                   at the moment the marker was FOUND. An HTTP body is
#                   routinely larger than curl's 4 KB stdio chunk, so a
#                   marker near the top of the response inverts reliably.
#                   That false negative restarts a HEALTHY service
#                   (your-org/nexus-code#622);
#                 * a cryptographic identity (monitor/remote-ssh-health.sh
#                   compares the live endpoint's SSH host key against ours).
#               A bare `curl -fsS -o /dev/null http://localhost:PORT/` is a
#               LIVENESS PROXY. It is better than nothing and fine for a
#               service whose false-green costs little — but know that is
#               what you are choosing, and prefer a marker grep in the
#               capture-then-match form above: it closes the whole class.
#
#               `pgrep`-based checks are namespace-scoped (so a foreign
#               process cannot satisfy them) but carry their own trap: an
#               `-f` pattern matches ANY process whose argv contains the
#               string, and an agent's argv carries its entire prompt. Scope
#               such checks by cwd/workdir and refuse on ambiguity
#               (your-org/nexus-code#608).
#   logfile     OPTIONAL. The service's logfile. It serves two readers:
#               (1) the cockpit `monitor/svc.sh` tails it (dashboard
#               picks and the `logs <name>` verb), and
#               (2) `bootstrap-recover.sh` appends a HEADLESS launch's
#               stdout/stderr here when it (re)starts the service. `~`
#               and `$NEXUS_ROOT` are expanded; a relative path resolves
#               under <workdir>. Omit it and both fall back to
#               <workdir>/serve.log.
#   policy      OPTIONAL. The watcher's continuous service-health task
#               (monitor/watcher/_service_health.sh) reads this to decide
#               WHAT it does when the healthcheck fails past the self-heal
#               grace window. Two values:
#                 auto-restart  (default) grace → minimal-downtime restart
#                               (flap-controlled) → emit. Use for services
#                               where a blind restart is safe.
#                 emit-only     never auto-restart; after grace the watcher
#                               escalates to the orchestrator and lets it
#                               decide. Use where a blind restart is unsafe
#                               or wants human judgment (e.g. a service
#                               with side effects on relaunch, or one whose
#                               failure usually signals a deeper problem).
#               Omit it to inherit monitor.service_health.default_policy
#               (auto-restart). REQUIRES the 5th <logfile> field present
#               (give an explicit logfile, or leave it empty between two
#               TABs) — it is the 6th positional column.
#               NOTE: only the service-health task consumes this; bootstrap-
#               recover / svc.sh / version-restart ignore it (they read it
#               into a throwaway var so it can't corrupt their logfile).
#
# THE COCKPIT'S DETAIL COLUMN — declare your endpoint, do not spell your
# healthcheck for it (your-org/nexus-code#1144). `monitor/svc.sh` shows a
# copy-pasteable URL in DETAIL. It reads that URL from the healthcheck COMMAND
# TEXT, which means the cell is a property of HOW THE HEALTHCHECK IS SPELLED
# rather than of the service: an inline `curl -fsS … http://localhost:PORT/`
# renders a URL, and a SCRIPT healthcheck renders `-`, indistinguishable from a
# service that genuinely serves nothing.
#
# That inverts the incentive, because a script is usually the BETTER probe and
# is sometimes the only correct one — an auth-gated service would see 401 from a
# bare `curl -fsS`, report unhealthy forever and restart-loop. So do NOT weaken a
# healthcheck to get a URL in the cockpit. Instead drop the URL in your service's
# own tree:
#
#     <workdir>/.deploy/endpoint      one line, one http(s) URL
#
# svc.sh reads it when the healthcheck names no URL, and applies the same
# external-bind rewrite (localhost -> FQDN when the port is bound beyond
# loopback) that makes the cell copy-pasteable. A line that is not URL-shaped is
# REFUSED and the cell shows `-` — the file's bytes reach a terminal, so shape is
# validated rather than mere non-emptiness. Display-only: nothing about this file
# changes what is probed, and healthcheck commands are never rewritten.
#
# Lines beginning with `#` and blank lines are ignored. A line with
# fewer than the four required TAB fields is skipped with a warning,
# never fatal. 4-field rows remain valid — the 5th + 6th fields are additive.
#
# Validate your edits without launching anything:
#     monitor/bootstrap-recover.sh --list      # echo parsed records
#     monitor/bootstrap-recover.sh --dry-run    # decide + log, no launch
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXAMPLE ENTRIES (this operator's stack as of 2026-06-09). Uncomment +
# adjust paths in your real `monitor/services.registry`. The columns
# below are TAB-separated; if you copy them, make sure your editor
# keeps the tabs. The trailing 5th column is the optional <logfile>; the
# 6th is the optional <policy> (auto-restart | emit-only).
#
# mysite-serve   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website   ./serve-supervised.sh   curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 3 http://localhost:8765/   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website/serve.log
#
# Same service, but pinned emit-only (watcher emits + lets the orchestrator
# decide instead of auto-restarting — the 6th column):
# mysite-serve   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website   ./serve-supervised.sh   curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 3 http://localhost:8765/   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website/serve.log   emit-only
#
# JupyterLab services (jupyter-*) are NOT hand-written here: run
# `monitor/jupyter-up.sh <project-dir>` and it appends/maintains the
# row itself (and `--down` removes it). A maintained row looks like:
# jupyter-myproj   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproj   /abs/path/monitor/labsh-supervised.sh   /abs/path/monitor/jupyter-health.sh   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproj/.jupyter/labsh-service.log
#
# DO NOT COPY THIS ROW (your-org/nexus-code#1222). It is kept, struck through,
# because it is what an operator registry actually contains today and the fix
# is only findable next to the mistake:
#
#   deploy-watch  …/website  ./deploy-watch.sh  pgrep -f deploy-watch.sh  …/deploy.log
#                                               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
# `claude`s argv IS its prompt, and an agent whose prompt merely QUOTES the
# script name satisfies that pattern. Measured on a live board by reading
# /proc/*/cmdline directly: FIVE processes matched `deploy-watch.sh`, of which
# exactly ONE was the service and TWO were live agents in other tmux windows.
# So if the real service died, the healthcheck would still go green on an
# agent alone: no incident opened, no relaunch, nothing emitted.
#
# This is NOT the #891 self-match, which was fixed by running the probe via
# `bash <(printf …)` so the pattern is no longer in the probe process own argv.
# This is the SIBLING match (#1073), and no spelling fixes it — `[d]eploy-watch`
# included — because the population that matches GROWS WITH EVERY WORKER SPAWNED
# and is authored by agents with no relationship to this predicate.
#
# Ask instead whether THE PROCESS YOU RECORDED is still alive. The supervisor
# writes its pid; `kill -0` answers about that pid and nothing else:
# deploy-watch    $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website   ./deploy-watch.sh   test -f .deploy-watch.pid && kill -0 "$(cat .deploy-watch.pid)" 2>/dev/null   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myproject/website/deploy.log
#
# A heartbeat-freshness check is the other sound form, and is what a sibling
# service in this workspace already uses: the supervisor touches a stamp each
# loop, and the healthcheck asserts the stamp is younger than one interval.
# Both ask about a thing; `pgrep -f` asks about a STRING, and a string cannot
# distinguish the thing from a description of the thing.
# myviewer        $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myviewer   ./run-supervised.sh   curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 3 http://localhost:8766/   $NEXUS_ROOT/work/myviewer/myviewer.log
#
# The confined remote SSH endpoint (agent-channel RFC Part A) is NOT
# hand-written either: run `monitor/remote-up.sh` and it appends/maintains
# the row itself (`--down` removes it). For this service, PRESENCE OF THE ROW
# IS THE ENABLE SIGNAL — there is no separate config flag (RFC §4.8). It is
# pinned `emit-only` (6th column) — a network listener is never
# blind-restarted; a wedged sshd escalates to the orchestrator via
# `--- service health ---`. With no row the launch wrapper exits without
# listening and the healthcheck treats not-running as healthy. A maintained
# row looks like (TAB-separated):
# nexus-remote-ssh   $NEXUS_ROOT   /abs/path/monitor/remote-sshd-supervised.sh   /abs/path/monitor/remote-ssh-health.sh   $NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/.state/remote-ssh.log   emit-only
# tmpfs guard — TWO rows, and only the read-only one is on by default
# (your-org/nexus-code#1441). `tmpfs-check` REPORTS: its launch loops
# `--check` and never deletes anything; its healthcheck goes non-zero when
# /tmp crosses a threshold, which escalates through `--- service health ---`
# to the orchestrator — the surface the operator already watches. It is
# `emit-only`: a reporter is never blind-restarted. The row exits non-zero
# when unhealthy; the supervisor reads the SCRIPT's status, never a
# pipeline's (`--check | tail -5` reports tail's rc — #1424).
tmpfs-check	$NEXUS_ROOT	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/tmpfs-guard.sh --check-daemon	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/tmpfs-guard.sh --check --quiet	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/.state/tmpfs-guard.log	emit-only
# `tmpfs-reap` DELETES: `--daemon` loops `--reap` under the default-deny
# predicate over the family list `REAP_FAMILIES_DEFAULT` in
# monitor/tmpfs-guard.sh — well-chosen for the nexus it was measured on, a
# GUESS about anyone else's /tmp. Deliberately COMMENTED OUT here: enabling it
# means reading that list first, and presence of the row is the enable signal
# (the same convention nexus-remote-ssh documents above). Uncomment to opt in.
# tmpfs-reap	$NEXUS_ROOT	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/tmpfs-guard.sh --daemon	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/tmpfs-guard.sh --check --quiet	$NEXUS_ROOT/monitor/.state/tmpfs-guard.log
